V1 -- the acceleration census of voids, analytic, across the OBSERVED size range
================================================================================================================
    g(R) = (4/3) pi G rho_m |delta| R at the edge of a spherical under-density
    rho_m = 2.6879e-27 kg/m^3 (Planck 2018 Omega_m = 0.315, h = 0.674)

     R (Mpc/h)    g (m/s^2)   y = g/a0 can   nu can      y alt   nu alt
            10    2.752e-13        0.00294    18.95    0.00244    20.75
            15    4.128e-13        0.00441    15.56    0.00366    17.03
            20    5.504e-13        0.00588    13.55    0.00488    14.82
            30    8.257e-13        0.00882    11.16    0.00732    12.20
            50    1.376e-12        0.01470     8.76    0.01220     9.56

    for comparison, the deepest point the RAR is actually measured at: SPARC outermost
    median g_bar/a_0 = 0.02 (k01_acceleration_census.out), nu = 7.58
    NOTE the direction: g grows with R at fixed delta, so the LARGEST voids are the
    SHALLOWEST in MOND terms and the smallest voids are the most enhanced.
  [PASS] V1 [EVERY OBSERVED VOID SIZE IS DEEPER IN THE MOND REGIME THAN ANYWHERE THE RAR IS MEASURED] the edge acceleration of a spherical under-density with the observed central contrast is computed across the observed radius range on both footings and the largest resulting y = g/a_0 is compared with the deep-MOND threshold y < 0.1 (nu > 4.1)
           (the worst case over R = 10-50 Mpc/h and both footings is y = 0.0147, threshold 0.1; the kernel boost runs nu = 8.8 to 18.9 (canonical), against nu = 7.6 at the deepest point SPARC reaches. Voids are the maximally-MOND environment, and this confirms k01's single literature scalar across the whole size range)

